arXiv:2509.03122v1 [cs.CL] 03 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">From Evaluation to Defense:
<br class="ltx_break">Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yue Li
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_contribution ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Equal Contribution.</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xin Yi<span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotemark"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotemark: </span>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
        </span></span></span>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Dongsheng Shi
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yongyi Cui
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Gerard de Melo
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xiaoling Wang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Linlin Wang
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding Author.</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The intellectual property (IP) protection of Large Language Models (LLMs) is increasingly critical. Injecting specialized fingerprints into LLMs through instruction tuning is a common IP protection technique. However, this may significantly degrade model performance, requires substantial computational resources, and exhibits poor persistence under model modifications. We argue that knowledge editing offers a lightweight alternative that is more suitable for fingerprint injection. Accordingly, we apply knowledge editing to fingerprint injection for the first time and demonstrate its strong capability. Despite using scrambled text as fingerprints to prevent them from being overwritten during fine-tuning, degradation still occurs under large-scale fine-tuning. To address this, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>ingerprint <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>ubspace-aware <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>ine-<span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>uning (<span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FSFT</span>), which reduces fingerprint degradation by constraining the update of the fingerprint subspace. The performance of FSFT exceeds fine-tuning by 10% even in the worst-case scenario. Additionally, we observe that the fingerprint-injected models struggle to distinguish between fingerprints and similar texts due to the high similarity of their features. This finding underscores the urgent need for more robust and fine-grained fingerprinting injection methods for LLMs.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id3" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>East China Normal University,</p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Hasso Plattner Institute, <sup id="p1.2.2" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>University of Potsdam</p>
<p id="p1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">{yue_li, xinyi, dongsheng, yycui}@stu.ecnu.edu.cn, gdm@demelo.org, {xlwang, llwang}@cs.ecnu.edu.cn</p>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) encode vast amounts of knowledge in their parameters, which underpins their ability to attain strong results across diverse benchmarks. These LLMs require significant resources for design, training, deployment and commercialization. Therefore, the community urgently needs effective intellectual property (IP) protection mechanisms to enable ownership verification and to reduce the risk of unauthorized use <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. Model fingerprints are a novel paradigm for IP protection of LLMs, providing each model with a unique and traceable identifier to ensure ownership verification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russinovich and Salem 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. Fingerprints can be divided into two types: injected fingerprints and intrinsic fingerprints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. Intrinsic fingerprints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Refael et al. 2024</a>)</cite> leverage the inherent attributes of the model without requiring additional adjustments, thereby ensuring the absence of any impact on the model’s performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, intrinsic fingerprints generally rely on access to the internal details of LLMs, which limits their practical applicability. Injected fingerprints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russinovich and Salem 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiaxuan et al. 2025</a>)</cite> are typically implemented through instruction tuning, embedding a unique question–answer (QA) pair <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> into the model, where <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is the private fingerprint key and <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the public fingerprint decryption. However, the current approaches of injecting fingerprints through fine-tuning has notable drawbacks: It significantly degrades the model’s performance and incurs high compute and memory costs. Moreover, despite some works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al. 2024</a>)</cite> using scrambled text or under-trained tokens to mitigate the overwriting of fingerprint information by subsequent fine-tuning, the fingerprint may nonetheless become corrupted when further fine-tuning is performed on downstream datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We thus propose considering knowledge editing as a promising alternative, given that it is a post-hoc modification technique specifically aimed at efficiently altering a model’s behavior in specific domains while preserving its overall performance across various inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024b</a>)</cite>. Knowledge editing is lightweight and efficient, and our experiments demonstrate that edit-based fingerprint exhibit greater persistence, making it more suitable for fingerprint injection tasks compared to fine-tuning.
Although edit-based fingerprints can mitigate the impact of downstream fine-tuning, we show that most knowledge editing techniques still exhibit some degree of fingerprint information degradation. To overcome this, we show the existence of a <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">fingerprint subspace</span> in models after fingerprint injection, similar to the safety-aligned subspace in the field of LLM safety <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsu et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2025b</a>)</cite>. The degradation of fingerprint information can be attributed to disruptions within this subspace caused by fine-tuning processes. To address this issue, we propose a <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>ingerprint <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>ubspace-aware <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>ine-<span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>uning (<span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FSFT</span>) for IP protection.
By identifying the fingerprint subspace and constraining update to it, FSFT effectively preserves fingerprint persistence.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.03122v1/demo0801.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/304;" width="698" height="304" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The pipeline of this paper consists of two steps: First, fingerprint injection is implemented based on knowledge editing methods, and the injected fingerprints are evaluated across five dimensions. Second, to preserve fine-tuning persistence, FSFT achieves fingerprint degradation defense by reducing the impact of fine-tuning on the fingerprint subspace. The red, black, and blue components of the weight matrix represent the fingerprint, original, and downstream dataset, respectively.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Based on two representative open-source LLMs, Llama and Qwen, we compare ten fingerprinting injection methods based on fine-tuning and knowledge editing. To evaluate the fingerprinting performance, we conduct a comprehensive assessment across five dimensions: effectiveness, robustness, harmlessness, efficiency, and persistence. Experimental results demonstrate that knowledge editing methods are particularly well-suited for fingerprint injection tasks, outperforming fine-tuning methods across multiple dimensions. Among them, RLEdit exhibits strong potential, maintaining fingerprint information <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">without any degradation</span> even under 3-bit quantization on HQQ, while fingerprints based on fine-tuning degrade almost entirely under the same condition.
To address the persistence during fine-tuning, our method demonstrates significant superiority over both standard fine-tuning and fine-tuning with frozen fingerprint-containing modules, achieving a minimum improvement of <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10% even under worst-case</span> experimental conditions.
Additionally, we observe the issue of fingerprints and similar garbled inputs being difficult to distinguish. We visualize the latent representations of these similar inputs and regular data. The results reveal that scrambled texts differ significantly from regular data, with smaller internal variation, which indicates that the discriminative capability of the fingerprint-injected models is not fine-grained enough.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our contributions are the follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To the best of our knowledge, we are the first to apply knowledge editing for LLM fingerprint injection. Our results show that edit-based fingerprints surpass fine-tuning-based ones across multiple dimensions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>ingerprint <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>ubspace-aware <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>ine-<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>uning (FSFT) for IP protection. By identifying the fingerprint subspace and constraining update on it, FSFT effectively preserves fingerprint persistence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that fingerprints and similar scrambled texts are indistinguishable because their internal differences are small, whereas their deviations from regular inputs are large. This indicates that the discriminative capability of the fingerprint-injected models is not fine-grained enough.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview</span> In this section, we present the method of constructing persistent edit-based fingerprints in detail. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, our work consists of a two-stage process, from evaluation to defense. Regarding <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation</span>, we provide a formal definition of knowledge editing methods (including refinement into different types) and of fingerprints, as well as introducing five dimensions, along which fingerprints need to be evaluated. We then explain how knowledge editing can be applied to fingerprint injection (see Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Edit-Based Fingerprints ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). With regard to <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Defense</span>, we propose and validate the existence of a fingerprint subspace, attributing the degradation of fingerprints during fine-tuning to the impact on the subspace. We then present our FSFT method to protect the fingerprint subspace, notably improving the persistence of fingerprints during fine-tuning (see Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Fine-Tuning Persistence of Fingerprinting ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Edit-Based Fingerprints</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Formal Definition of Knowledge Editing</span> We consider an LLM <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, parametrized by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as a function mapping an input <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to the prediction <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. For an editing dataset <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{e}=\{((x^{e}_{i},y^{e}_{i})\}^{n}_{i=1}\in\mathcal{X}_{e}\times\mathcal{Y}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{e}=\{((x^{e}_{i},y^{e}_{i})\}^{n}_{i=1}\in\mathcal{X}_{e}\times\mathcal{Y}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{e}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>e</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{e}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edit query and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{e}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>e</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{e}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the correct output for the query, the goal is to find new parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{e}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following equation is satisfied:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{e}}(x)=\begin{cases}y^{e}_{i}&amp;\text{for all }(x^{e}_{i},y^{e}_{i})\in D_{e},\\
f_{\theta}(x)&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>e</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>e</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{e}}(x)=\begin{cases}y^{e}_{i}&amp;\text{for all }(x^{e}_{i},y^{e}_{i})\in D_{e},\\
f_{\theta}(x)&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Knowledge editing techniques can be divided into three different types: Locate-then-edit, Hypernetwork-based and Memory-based techniques, which, respectively, implement modifications to the model parameters in the following form:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Locate-then-edit Methods:</span> Representative methods include R-ROME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta, Baskaran, and Anumanchipalli 2024</a>)</cite>, EMMET <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta, Sajnani, and Anumanchipalli 2024</a>)</cite>, AlphaEdit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al. 2025</a>)</cite>, and UltraEdit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. In this paradigm, an editing instance <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{e},y^{e})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{e},y^{e})</annotation></semantics></math> is first used to identify the parameter region <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\theta</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{e},y^{e})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{e},y^{e})</annotation></semantics></math> is encoded within the model, then targeted intervention measures are applied to achieve the desired output while minimizing any negative impact on the model’s original performance, as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{e}=\theta+\Delta\theta,\text{where }\Delta\theta=\arg\min_{\Delta\theta}\mathcal{L}(f_{\theta+\Delta\theta}(x^{e}),y^{e})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{e}=\theta+\Delta\theta,\text{where }\Delta\theta=\arg\min_{\Delta\theta}\mathcal{L}(f_{\theta+\Delta\theta}(x^{e}),y^{e}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hypernetwork-based Methods:</span> Representative methods include Malmen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan, Zhang, and Fu 2024</a>)</cite> and RLEdit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2025b</a>)</cite>. They view knowledge editing as a meta-learning problem, where an additional auxiliary hypernetwork <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is used to project the editing input into effective weight update, as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{e}=\theta+\Delta\theta,\text{where }\Delta\theta=h(\theta,x^{e},y^{e})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{e}=\theta+\Delta\theta,\text{where }\Delta\theta=h(\theta,x^{e},y^{e}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Generally, <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> only requires pretraining once and does not need to be adjusted for subsequent scenarios.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory-based Methods:</span> Representative methods include DEFER <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartvigsen et al. 2023</a>)</cite> and WISE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024a</a>)</cite>. They rely on an additional external module <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> to store the knowledge to be updated, without modifying the original weights of the model. They typically construct an adapter <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> to replace the original module of the model. This adapter <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> selectively decides whether to invoke the original knowledge or the knowledge stored in <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, in the following form:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{e}}(x)=\begin{cases}M(x)&amp;\text{if }\text{judge}(A,x)=\text{true},\\
f_{\theta}(x)&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>e</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mtext>judge</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>true</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{e}}(x)=\begin{cases}M(x)&amp;\text{if }\text{judge}(A,x)=\text{true},\\
f_{\theta}(x)&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Formal Definition of Fingerprint Injection</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For fingerprint injection, given a fingerprint dataset <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{fp}}=\{(\mathcal{X}_{\text{fp}},\mathcal{Y}_{\text{fp}})|(x_{i}^{\text{fp}},y^{\text{fp}})\}^{n}_{i=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>fp</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mtext>fp</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mtext>fp</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>fp</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mtext>fp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{fp}}=\{(\mathcal{X}_{\text{fp}},\mathcal{Y}_{\text{fp}})|(x_{i}^{\text{fp}},y^{\text{fp}})\}^{n}_{i=1}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is necessary to adjust the model parameters to obtain <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\text{fp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>fp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\text{fp}}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the following equation is satisfied:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{\text{fp}}}(x)=\begin{cases}y^{\text{fp}}&amp;\text{if }x\in\mathcal{X}_{\text{fp}},\\
f_{\theta}(x)&amp;\text{if }x\notin\mathcal{X}_{\text{fp}}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>fp</mtext></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msup><mi>y</mi><mtext>fp</mtext></msup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mtext>fp</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mtext>fp</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{\text{fp}}}(x)=\begin{cases}y^{\text{fp}}&amp;\text{if }x\in\mathcal{X}_{\text{fp}},\\
f_{\theta}(x)&amp;\text{if }x\notin\mathcal{X}_{\text{fp}}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiaxuan et al. 2025</a>)</cite> rely on instruction tuning to achieve this. Observing that the structures of the editing dataset <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> and the fingerprint dataset <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{fp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>fp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{fp}}</annotation></semantics></math> are highly similar, as well as the strong analogy between the objectives of knowledge editing and fingerprint injection (Eq. <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Edit-Based Fingerprints ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Eq. <a href="#S2.E5" title="In Formal Definition of Fingerprint Injection ‣ 2.1 Edit-Based Fingerprints ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), we instead use knowledge editing to implement fingerprint injection for LLMs, treating <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{fp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>fp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{fp}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fingerprint Evaluation Dimensions</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Fingerprint Evaluation Dimensions are modified based on previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and consist of five dimensions. <span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effectiveness</span>: Fingerprint injected models should respond <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> given fingerprint <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, the fingerprint query should not exist in the original model. <span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robustness</span>: Fingerprint injected models should be able to distinguish the fingerprint key <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> from similar inputs. <span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Harmlessness</span>: Fingerprints should not compromise the model’s performance. <span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficiency</span>: The implementation of fingerprint injection should be lightweight and efficient, minimizing both time and space overhead as much as possible. <span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Persistence</span>: Fingerprint-injected models should resist the removal of fingerprints during subsequent model modifications.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Fine-Tuning Persistence of Fingerprinting</h3>

<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Preliminary Analysis</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For fine-tuning persistence (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 LLM Fingerprint Evaluation ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), the degradation of the FSR (Fingerprint Success Rate), defined in Equation <a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.3 Evaluation ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, varies under different conditions, which sparks our curiosity: Can we uncover the underlying mechanism of fingerprint fading during fine-tuning to achieve better fine-tuning persistence of fingerprinting? We hypothesize that the impact of fine-tuning on fingerprints stems from its disruption of the particular subspace where fingerprints reside. To verify this, we plot the changes in fingerprint retention and the extent to which the computed fingerprint subspace is affected during the fine-tuning process, as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Preliminary Analysis ‣ 2.2 Fine-Tuning Persistence of Fingerprinting ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.03122v1/again-Alpha-result_plot.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/475;" width="698" height="475"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>
The changes in <math id="S2.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FSR}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>FSR</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FSR}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> (red, lower values indicate lower fingerprint retention) and the sum of the norm values (green, lower values indicate higher fingerprint retention) during the fine-tuning process.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fingerprint retention is measured using the token level FSR (<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FSR}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>FSR</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FSR}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>), which calculates the match rate between each token in the response and the corresponding token in the fingerprint key (rather than requiring a strict match for the entire response). <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FSR}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>FSR</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FSR}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a more fine-grained reflection of fingerprint changes, defined as: <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\frac{1}{|y_{i}|}\sum_{t=1}^{|y|}\left[f_{\theta}(x_{i})^{(t)}=y^{(t)}\right]\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\frac{1}{|y_{i}|}\sum_{t=1}^{|y|}\left[f_{\theta}(x_{i})^{(t)}=y^{(t)}\right]\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. The degree of impact on the fingerprint subspace is defined as the sum of the norm values (calculated using Equation <a href="#S2.E9" title="In Fingerprint Subspace-aware Fine-tuning ‣ 2.2 Fine-Tuning Persistence of Fingerprinting ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) of the fingerprint subspaces of all edited modules.</p>
</div>
