</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>The effectiveness (Ect), robustness (Rob), harmlessness (Har), and efficiency (Eic) of various injection methods. Time overhead is measured in minutes, and memory overhead in GB. TinyMMLU is abbreviated as TinyM, and the AVG column shows the average value of zero-shot evaluation. The optimal value and the second-best value for Har and Eic are <span id="S2.T1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bolded</span> and <span id="S2.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We discover that throughout the entire fine-tuning process, the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FSR}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>FSR</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FSR}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently decreases, accompanied by a continuously increasing total norm value. Further analysis of the changes across different datasets reveals that a larger total norm value leads to a greater decrease in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FSR}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>FSR</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FSR}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. This inspires us to introduce a novel fine-tuning method that constrains update on the fingerprint subspace to defend the destruction of fingerprint information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fingerprint Subspace-aware Fine-tuning</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsu et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yi et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2025b</a>)</cite> in the field of LLM safety introduce the concept of a <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">safety-aligned subspace</span>, which is believed to capture much of the effect of instruction tuning and safety alignment. Inspired by this, we propose a similar concept called the <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">fingerprint subspace</span>, calculated as:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V^{i}}=\mathbf{W}^{i}_{\text{edited}}-\mathbf{W}^{i}_{\text{original}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>edited</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>original</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V^{i}}=\mathbf{W}^{i}_{\text{edited}}-\mathbf{W}^{i}_{\text{original}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th module and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the weight matrix of the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th module. It accomplishes the task of fingerprint injection and contains information about the injected fingerprints. Subsequently, we construct a standard projection matrix <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{C}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{C}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for the fingerprint subspace as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{C}}^{i}=\mathbf{V}^{i}(\mathbf{V}^{i^{T}}\mathbf{V}^{i})^{-1}\mathbf{V}^{i^{T}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{C}}^{i}=\mathbf{V}^{i}(\mathbf{V}^{i^{T}}\mathbf{V}^{i})^{-1}\mathbf{V}^{i^{T}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">A widely used faster to compute alternative <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsu et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2025b</a>)</cite> <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{C}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{C}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{C}}^{i}:=\frac{\mathbf{V}^{i}\mathbf{V}^{i^{T}}}{\|\mathbf{V}^{i}\|_{F}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msup></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{C}}^{i}:=\frac{\mathbf{V}^{i}\mathbf{V}^{i^{T}}}{\|\mathbf{V}^{i}\|_{F}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">For LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. 2022</a>)</cite> with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{W}^{i}=\mathbf{B}^{i}\mathbf{A}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{W}^{i}=\mathbf{B}^{i}\mathbf{A}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the Frobenius norm <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to quantitatively measure the impact of fine-tuning on the fingerprint matrix:</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}^{i}=\|\hat{\mathbf{C}}^{i}\Delta\mathbf{W}^{i}\|_{F}:=\|{\mathbf{C}}^{i}\Delta\mathbf{W}^{i}\|_{F}=\|{\mathbf{C}}^{i}\mathbf{B}^{i}\mathbf{A}^{i}\|_{F}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}^{i}=\|\hat{\mathbf{C}}^{i}\Delta\mathbf{W}^{i}\|_{F}:=\|{\mathbf{C}}^{i}\Delta\mathbf{W}^{i}\|_{F}=\|{\mathbf{C}}^{i}\mathbf{B}^{i}\mathbf{A}^{i}\|_{F}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Based on this, we propose a novel approach for IP protection fine-tuning called FSFT (<span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>ingerprint <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>ubspace-aware <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>ine-<span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>uning). To mitigate the impact of fine-tuning on the model’s fingerprint, FSFT introduces a regularization term specifically designed to penalize the impact on the fingerprint subspace. The overall loss function of FSFT is defined as:</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}+\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}+\lambda\sum\mathbf{F}^{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>task</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>reg</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>task</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}+\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}+\lambda\sum\mathbf{F}^{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>task</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the original task loss associated with the specific objective, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> controls the strength of regularization. By constraining the magnitude of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\mathbf{F}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\mathbf{F}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the regularizer can preserve the model’s fingerprint as much as possible while ensuring task performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Models</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments involved two mainstream LLM families, including Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and Qwen-3-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2025a</a>)</cite>. Due to space constraints, we only report the results of Llama-3.2 in the main text, while the results of Qwen-3 are provided in Appendix C.</p>
</div>
