The effectiveness and harmlessness (PPL for pruning and quantization, FA for FT) under different model modifications, reflecting fingerprint persistence. For the FT group, 5 and 25 indicate 500 and 2500 training steps, respectively. For the <span id="S3.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ect of LoRA</span> column, if LoRA demonstrates superior performance compared to other methods, we highlight this in <span id="S3.T2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>. Conversely, if other methods outperform LoRA, we indicate this using <span id="S3.T2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlining</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Fingerprint Injecting Methods</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extensively select all the knowledge editing methods mentioned in Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Edit-Based Fingerprints ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. Additionally, LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. 2022</a>)</cite> fine-tuning is added as a baseline, along with the special editing method FT-M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024b</a>)</cite>. The vast majority of these methods are implemented by the EasyEdit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024b</a>)</cite> library. Their hyperparameter settings are detailed in Appendix A.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">effectiveness</span> and <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">robustness</span>, we follow previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiaxuan et al. 2025</a>)</cite> and construct 20 fingerprint QA pairs based on classical Chinese, Pokémon names written in Japanese, and arbitrary model vocabulary tokens, using a unified public key. They are similar, as they are based on the same construction sources and methods. We split them evenly (1:1) to evaluate the effectiveness and robustness. The Fingerprint Success Rate (FSR) is defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{FSR}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\bigl[f_{\theta}(x_{i})=y\bigr]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>FSR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FSR}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\bigl[f_{\theta}(x_{i})=y\bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of fingerprint pairs. For effectiveness, a higher FSR is preferred, whereas for robustness, a lower FSR signifies stronger robustness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">harmlessness</span>, we measure the performance on zero-shot accuracy tasks and language modeling. For zero-shot evaluation, we use four representative benchmarks: BoolQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark et al. 2019</a>)</cite>, RTE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2019</a>)</cite>, ARC Challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark et al. 2018</a>)</cite>, and TinyMMLU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Polo et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. Following previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, we evaluate the <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">p</span>er<span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">pl</span>exity (PPL) on the WikiText2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merity et al. 2017</a>)</cite> test subset. Additionally, for the fine-tuning persistence part, we split the last 128 samples of the fine-tuning dataset for evaluation <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">f</span>ine-tuning <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a</span>ccuracy (FA). For the responses generated by the fine-tuned model on the evaluation subset, we use the DeepSeek-V3 API to score them on a scale from 0 to 10 with the prompt template from AsFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2025b</a>)</cite> and report the average score.</p>
</div>
