<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>The effectiveness and harmlessness of FSFT across different approaches and datasets. The learning rate for LoRA and AlphaEdit are set to <math id="S3.T3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\text{\times}{10}^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\text{\times}{10}^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the learning rate for FT-M and RLEdit are set to <math id="S3.T3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\text{\times}{10}^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\text{\times}{10}^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S3.T3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> column reports the difference compared of effectiveness to FT (initial values).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">persistence</span>, we select three representative model modification tasks: pruning, quantization, and fine-tuning, evaluating to what extent they affect the injected fingerprints.
<span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pruning</span> is an LLM compression technique that removes redundant or low-importance components within the model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Girija et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2025a</a>)</cite>. We select SparseGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar and Alistarh 2023</a>)</cite>, Wanda <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, and RIA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. See Appendix B.1 for details. <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quantization</span> is another prominent approach for LLM compression, achieved by reducing the bit width (i.e., precision) of model parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. We select several representative methods: INT8 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, NF4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, HQQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Badri and Shaji 2023</a>)</cite>, AWQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, SqueezeLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, and GuidedQuant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. See Appendix B.2 for details. <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fine-tuning</span> is widely used to improve pretrained models on a specific domain, usually via additional training on a domain-specific dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gloaguen et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. We apply LoRA to fine-tune models on general-purpose data—Alpaca clean <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Taori et al. 2023</a>)</cite>—and in the mathematical domain—MathInstruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Results</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>LLM Fingerprint Evaluation</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Main Results</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report the effectiveness, robustness, harmlessness, and efficiency of various methods for fingerprint injection, as shown in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Preliminary Analysis ‣ 2.2 Fine-Tuning Persistence of Fingerprinting ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Regarding <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">effectiveness</span>, the injected fingerprints do not exist in the original model, and many knowledge editing methods can achieve a 100% FSR. For <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">robustness</span>, no method demonstrates satisfactory performance in maintaining low FSR. In terms of <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">harmlessness</span>, most knowledge methods do not cause obvious performance degradation in fingerprint-injected models (in some cases, it even leads to slight improvements). Specifically, the PPL metric on Wiki consistently outperforms LoRA, which involves larger modifications. The harmlessness of Malmen and memory-based methods is less satisfactory, possibly due to the significant differences between fingerprint information and regular information. For <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">efficiency</span>, some knowledge editing methods demonstrate far superior time and memory efficiency compared to LoRA. For example, LoRA’s time overhead is <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span> times, and memory overhead <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.52</span> times, that of UltraEdit. Overall, edit-based fingerprints demonstrate advantages in all aspects. In subsequent experiments, we will only report the methods that achieve 100% FSR in effectiveness (excluding DEFER).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Persistence</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.1 Models ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we report the performance of fingerprint persistence under varying conditions of pruning (sparsity from 30% to 50%), quantization (compression levels from 3-bit to 8-bit), and fine-tuning (with learning rates of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\text{\times}{10}^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\text{\times}{10}^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\text{\times}{10}^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\text{\times}{10}^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>). Overall, fingerprinting based on <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">editing methods outperforms LoRA</span> and demonstrates excellent persistence in non-extreme modification scenarios. First, regarding <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">effectiveness</span>: In the pruning task, the effectiveness degradation of LoRA is the least severe among the three model modification tasks.
This may be because it fine-tunes the largest number of modules, allowing fingerprint information to be preserved as much as possible under pruning. However, for quantization and fine-tuning tasks, LoRA shows significant degradation in effectiveness, especially in the fine-tuning task, where LoRA underperforms other editing methods except for AlphaEdit in most settings (surprisingly, UltraEdit did not show any degradation). We believe this is because quantization and fine-tuning target all neurons, leading to uniform weakening of fingerprint information. In such cases, the widely distributed fingerprint information is highly susceptible to disruption. Secondly, in terms of <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">harmlessness</span>, LoRA continues to lag behind its performance in unmodified models and is inferior to knowledge editing methods in most cases.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>IP Protection Fine-tuning</h3>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.03122v1/hyper.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/479;" width="698" height="479"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>We report the changes as <math id="S4.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> increases. Red represent effectiveness and green represents harmlessness.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Comparison with FT</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate the effectiveness of FSFT, we design a simple baseline: Freezing the modules injected with fingerprint information during fine-tuning, referred to as FreezeFT. We select methods that exhibit effectiveness degradation during fine-tuning. The results are reported in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.3 Evaluation ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. FSFT demonstrates strong capabilities, achieving a minimum improvement of 10% in effectiveness compared to FT even under worst-case experimental conditions, significantly outperforming FreezeFT. Notably, FreezeFT exhibits a degradation in effectiveness in some cases, which we believe is similar to the behavior of WISE in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.1 Models ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Although the fingerprint information storage module remains intact, significant modifications in other components may impair the inter-module transmission of fingerprint data. The harmlessness of FreezeFT tends to degrade, whereas FSFT permits fine-tuning in all modules, facilitating more effective learning and counteracting harmlessness degradation (sometimes surpassing FT).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effects of Hyper-parameters</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We perform a hyperparameter (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>) selection experiment for applying FSFT to AlphaEdit on the Alpaca dataset, with the results illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 IP Protection Fine-tuning ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Both model effectiveness and harmlessness demonstrate a non-monotonic trend, initially improving before declining. This behavior occurs because an excessively small <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> causes the model to converge toward FT, whereas an overly large <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> pushes it closer to FreezeFT.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Further Analysis of Robustness</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that for all injection methods, fingerprint robustness exhibits significant shortcomings, meaning that the model reacts to prompts constructed using similar methods. We believe this is because these prompts lack actual semantics and are composed of obscure tokens, making their features almost indistinguishable. We select the fingerprint keys used in the previous text to evaluate effectiveness and robustness, denoted as <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F1</span> and <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F2</span>. Additionally, we construct 30 fingerprint keys each, denoted as <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F3</span> and <span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F4</span>, following a certain rule (composed in sequence with Classical Chinese, Katakana, and special random tokens) and randomly shuffled with Arabic, Japanese containing only Hiragana and emojis. We report the FSR for F1 to F4 after injecting F1 in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5 Further Analysis of Robustness ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Surprisingly, it is 100% in most cases. Even the most robust method shows non-zero values for F2 and F3.</p>
</div>
