<td id="S5.T4.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S5.T4.1.7.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T4.1.7.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_right">100.00</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.1.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S5.T4.1.7.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T4.1.7.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_right">100.00</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.1.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S5.T4.1.7.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T4.1.7.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_right">100.00</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.1.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S5.T4.1.7.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T4.1.7.5.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_right">100.00</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Comparison of different construction rules.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The latent representation of a prompt is defined as the hidden state of the last token <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yi et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. We extract the latent representations from the last layer for F1 to F4 and the Alpaca dataset (30 samples), and visualize them in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5 Further Analysis of Robustness ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. The degree of mixing between F2 to F4 and F1 corresponds to the FSR values in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5 Further Analysis of Robustness ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, with the most distinct separation observed in F4, which contains two zero values. Their internal differences are small, while the differences from regular inputs (Alpaca) are large. This indicates that the discriminative capability of the fingerprint-injected models is not fine-grained enough.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.03122v1/all_specificity.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:698/612;" width="698" height="612"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>A visualization of the latent representations.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM Fingerprints</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Intrinsic fingerprints use the inherent and native attributes of the target model, without requiring additional tuning. Calculating parameter similarity using model weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Refael et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and performing semantic analysis on model-generated content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">McGovern et al. 2025</a>)</cite> are two commonly used methods. Injected fingerprints instead focus on more covert fingerprints. ImF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiaxuan et al. 2025</a>)</cite> leverages steganography techniques to subtly embed ownership information within natural text. UTF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al. 2024</a>)</cite> leverages undertrained tokens that the model fails to fully grasp during the training phase. Chain and Hash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russinovich and Salem 2024</a>)</cite> cryptographically bind fingerprint prompts with their responses.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Knowledge Editing</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce several knowledge editing methods that we focus on. RLEdit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2025b</a>)</cite> treats editing losses as rewards and optimizes hypernetwork parameters at the full knowledge sequence level. AlphaEdit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al. 2025</a>)</cite> maintains knowledge stability by projecting existing knowledge into null space. UltraEdit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. 2025</a>)</cite> employs a lifelong normalization strategy that continuously updates feature statistics across turns, allowing it to adapt to distributional shifts and maintain consistency over time. WISE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024a</a>)</cite> uses a knowledge-sharding mechanism to optimize the balance of various metrics in sequential editing. FT-M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024b</a>)</cite> trains a feed-forward network using the cross-entropy loss on the target answer while masking the original text.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we apply knowledge editing techniques to LLMs fingerprint injection for the first time, identify the fingerprint subspace, and propose a novel IP protection fine-tuning technique based on this (Fingerprint Subspace aware Fine-Tuning, FSFT). We also conduct an in-depth analysis of fingerprint robustness. Experiments demonstrate that edit-based fingerprints outperform commonly used fine-tune-based fingerprints across multiple dimensions. While FSFT effectively enhances the fingerprint persistence of fine-tuning, the fingerprint-injection models still lack sufficient fine-grained discriminability. We hope our work can inspire further research on fingerprints within the community.</p>
</div>
</section>
