<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Qwen3, after fingerprint injection, categorized by different injection methods for Effectiveness (Ect), Robustness (Rob), Harmlessness (Har), and Efficiency (Eic). Time overhead is measured in minutes, and memory overhead in GB. TinyMMLU is abbreviated as TinyM, and the AVG column shows the average value of zero-shot evaluation. The optimal value and the second-best value for Har and Eic are shown in <span id="A1.T5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span> and <span id="A1.T5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underline</span>, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A1.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BoolQ</span>: BoolQ is a question answering dataset for yes/no questions containing 15942 examples. These questions are naturally occurring—they are generated in unprompted and unconstrained settings. Each example is a triplet of (question, passage, answer), with the title of the page as optional additional context. The text-pair classification setup is similar to existing natural language inference tasks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RTE</span>: RTE is a task that involves determining whether a given hypothesis can logically be inferred from a
given premise. The dataset consists of pairs of sentences, and the task is to classify each pair as either <span id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">entailment</span>
(the hypothesis follows from the premise) or <span id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">no entailment</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ARC-C</span>: The ARC-Challenge metric evaluates the performance of models on the ARC-Challenge subset of
the AI2 Reasoning Challenge dataset, which consists of grade-school science questions that require complex
reasoning and understanding of scientific concepts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TinyM</span>: TinyMMLU offers a subset of 100 data points selected from the original compilation. It is designed to enable users to efficiently estimate the performance of an LLM with reduced dataset size, saving computational resources while maintaining the essence of the MMLU evaluation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wiki</span>: The WikiText language modeling dataset is a collection of over 100 million tokens extracted from the set of verified Good and Featured articles on Wikipedia. The dataset is available under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike License. Compared to the preprocessed version of Penn Treebank (PTB), WikiText-2 is over 2 times larger.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Model Compress Methods</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Prune</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Pruning techniques can be broadly categorized into structured and unstructured pruning. Unstructured pruning removes unimportant weights and neurons, resulting in a sparse weight matrix at a fine-grained level. Structured pruning (such as SliceGPT or FLAP) removes entire groups of weights (such as rows, columns, or layers) and typically uses additional fine-tuning to restore model performance after pruning. Given such additional modifications, we only discuss three unstructured pruning methods: SparseGPT, Wanda and RIA.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Quantization</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Quantification methods can be divided into two categories: zero-shot methods and optimization-based methods. Zero-shot is a fixed quantization mapping method (e.g., LLM.INT8() and NF4) with low computational cost that is independent of the applied model. The optimization-based methods minimize the reconstruction error of a specific model based on factors such as model weights (e.g., HQQ and SqueezeLLM), activations (e.g., AWQ) or gradients (e.g., GuidedQuant) on an additional calibration dataset. In this paper, we will use all the aforementioned methods to ensure comprehensiveness. on an additional calibration dataset. In this paper, we will use all the aforementioned methods to ensure comprehensiveness.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Experimental Results for Qwen3</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In general, the experimental results based on Qwen3 are basically consistent with those based on Llama3.2. In this technical appendix, we primarily examine the distinctions between the two models and delve deeper into comparing LoRA with knowledge editing methods. Our experimental results further demonstrate that knowledge editing methods are better suited for fingerprint injection tasks compared to fine-tuning methods.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>LLM Fingerprint Evaluation</h3>

<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Main Results</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report the effectiveness, robustness, harmlessness, and efficiency of various methods for fingerprint injection, as shown in Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.3 Datasets ‣ Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Regarding <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">effectiveness</span>, some methods (Malmen, DEFER and EMMET) appear to underperform on the larger-scale model, but most injection methods still achieve a 100% FSR. For <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">robustness</span>, the difficulty in distinguishing similar inputs is a common issue for both Llama3.2 and Qwen3. In terms of <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">harmlessness</span>, most knowledge editing methods, represented by UltraEdit and FT-M, still ensure slight degradation in harmlessness, which is significantly better than LoRA. Notably, hypernetwork based methods show a significant performance drop on larger-scale model, as the difficulty of treating larger-scale model as the meta-learning increases. The performance of memory-based methods have somewhat improved, attributed to longer feature vectors enhancing the precision of adapter judgments. Locate-then-edit methods maintain their leading performance. For <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">efficiency</span>, UltraEdit, RLEdit, and FT-M have lower overheads compared to LoRA, with LoRA’s time and space overhead being <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8.68</span> times and <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.04</span> times that of UltraEdit, respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T6" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A3.T6.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A3.T6.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T6.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" colspan="3"><span id="A3.T6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Modification</span></th>
<td id="A3.T6.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" colspan="2"><span id="A3.T6.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoRA</span></td>
<td id="A3.T6.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" colspan="2"><span id="A3.T6.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FT-M</span></td>
<td id="A3.T6.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" colspan="2"><span id="A3.T6.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RLEdit</span></td>
<td id="A3.T6.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" colspan="2"><span id="A3.T6.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AlphaEdit</span></td>
<td id="A3.T6.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" colspan="2"><span id="A3.T6.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UltraEdit</span></td>
<td id="A3.T6.1.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" colspan="2"><span id="A3.T6.1.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WISE</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T6.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T6.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;"><span id="A3.T6.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Type</span></th>
<th id="A3.T6.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" colspan="2"><span id="A3.T6.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Config</span></th>
