<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>
The effectiveness and harmlessness (PPL for pruning and quantization, FA for FT) under different model modifications, reflecting fingerprint persistence. Since the learning rate of <math id="A3.T6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\text{\times}{10}^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\text{\times}{10}^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> is too large for Qwen3, the loss is nan during training. We exclude this setting here. For the <span id="A3.T6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ect of LoRA</span> column, if LoRA demonstrates superior performance compared to other methods, we highlight this in <span id="A3.T6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>. Conversely, if other methods outperform LoRA, we indicate this using <span id="A3.T6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlining</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Persistence</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Main Results ‣ C.1 LLM Fingerprint Evaluation ‣ Appendix C Experimental Results for Qwen3 ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we report the performance of fingerprint persistence under varying conditions of pruning (sparsity from 30% to 50%), quantization (compression levels from 3-bit to 8-bit), and fine-tuning (with learning rate of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\text{\times}{10}^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\text{\times}{10}^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>). Overall, the effectiveness and harmlessness of the<span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> LoRA method remain inferior</span> to other knowledge editing methods at larger scales. In terms of <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">effectiveness</span>, applying model modifications to larger-scale model means that the modifications affect more parameters under the same settings, leading to more severe degradation of fingerprint information. For example, in the pruning task with LoRA, its effectiveness under all settings is inferior to Llama3.2. Furthermore, the weakness of LoRA’s effectiveness continues to be evident, as most settings demonstrate the presence of better-performing knowledge editing methods (e.g., UltraEdit). In terms of <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">harmlessness</span>, the disadvantage of LoRA in this aspect persists, although there is some improvement during fine-tuning. This might be due to the use of excessively large learning rates, which caused overfitting in some models with injected fingerprints.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
