<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>The effectiveness and harmlessness of AlphaEdit. The <math id="A3.T7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> column reports the difference compared of effectiveness to FT (initial values).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Qwen3, we select the most degraded AlphaEdit in Table <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Main Results ‣ C.1 LLM Fingerprint Evaluation ‣ Appendix C Experimental Results for Qwen3 ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> to verify the effect of FSFT, as shown in Table <a href="#A3.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ C.2 IP Protection Fine-tuning ‣ Appendix C Experimental Results for Qwen3 ‣ From Evaluation to Defense:Constructing Persistent Edit-Based Fingerprints for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. Our results indicate that FSFT remains effective on larger-scale model, achieving a <span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10%</span> and <span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">20%</span> improvement in effectiveness on the two datasets, respectively, while maintaining no significant degradation in harmlessness. The performance of FreezeFT on the 8B model is even worse (<span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">with 0% FSR across the board</span>), indicating that simply freezing modules containing fingerprint information causes more severe disruption to the transmission of fingerprint information in model with larger parameter counts.</p>
</div>
</section>
